The area of the window determines how much light enters the building.
\begin{align*}
A&=A_{rectangle}+A_{halfCircle}\\
\intertext{Since the radius of the half-circle is half the width of the rectangle, $width=2r$,}
A_{rectangle}&=height\times width=h\cdot 2r\\
A_{halfCircle}&=\frac{1}{2}\pi r^2\\
\intertext{Thus,}
A&=2rh+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}
\end{align*}

The perimeter consists of $3$ sides of the rectangle and the length of the boundary of the half circle:
\begin{align*}
P&=h+2r+h+\pi r\\
P&=2h+2r+\pi r\\
\intertext{The perimeter must be $30$ feet, so}
30&=2h+2r+\pi r
\end{align*}

We maximize the amount of light, i.e. maximize area. $A$ depends on both $r$ and $h$, so reduce to one variable using the perimeter constraint. Solving for $h$ is easier:
\begin{align*}
2h+2r+\pi r&=30\\
2h &=30-2r-\pi r\\
h&=\frac{30-2r-\pi r}{2}\\
h&=\frac{30}{2}-\frac{2r}{2}-\frac{\pi r}{2}\\
h&=15-r-\frac{\pi r}{2}
\end{align*}
Substitute into $A$:
\begin{align*}
A&=2rh+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}\\
&=2r\left(15-r-\frac{\pi r}{2}\right)+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}\\
&=30r-2r^2-\pi r^2+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}\\
A(r)&=30r-2r^2-\frac{\pi r^2}{2}
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
A'(r)&=30-4r-\pi r\\
A'(r)&=30-r(4+\pi)
\end{align*}
There are two conditions that generate critical numbers:
\begin{itemize}
\item $A'(r)$ DNE: requires division by $0$, $\sqrt{(-)}$, $\ln(0)$, or $\ln(-)$. No value of $r$ creates any of these.
\item $A'(r)=0$:
\begin{align*}
A'(r)&=0\\
30-r(4+\pi)&=0\\
-r(4+\pi)&=-30\\
r&=\frac{30}{4+\pi}\approx 4.2\text{ feet}
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}

Domain: $r$ must be positive (a negative or zero radius makes no sense), so $0<r<\infty$. Only one critical number in the domain: $r=\frac{30}{4+\pi}\approx 4.2$ feet.

Sign line for $A'(r)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $r=3$ (left of $4.2$): $A'(3)=30-3(4+\pi)\approx +8.58>0$.
\item $r=5$ (right of $4.2$): $A'(5)=30-5(4+\pi)\approx -5.71<0$.
\end{itemize}
$A'$ goes $(+)\to(-)$ at $r\approx 4.2$, so $r\approx 4.2$ is a local maximum by the First Derivative Test. Since it is the only critical number on the domain and is a local maximum, the First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema gives an absolute maximum there.

\begin{align*}
r&=\frac{30}{4+\pi}\approx 4.2\text{ feet}\\
h&=15-r-\frac{\pi r}{2}\\
h&=15-\frac{30}{4+\pi}-\frac{\pi}{2}\cdot\frac{30}{4+\pi}\approx 4.2\text{ feet}
\end{align*}

problems.context_latex:
An architect is designing a large window for renovations in Clemons library. The window is composed of a rectangle and a half of a circular disc. The half-disc sits atop the rectangle so its diameter equals the width of the rectangle. Let $r$ be the radius of the half-circle (so the rectangle has width $2r$) and let $h$ be the height of the rectangle. To allow enough light into the library the window must have a total area of exactly $100$ feet$^2$. The cost of materials varies:
\begin{itemize}
\item The frame for the half-disc costs $\$20$ per foot.
\item The frame for the sides of the rectangle costs $\$10$ per foot.
\end{itemize}
Find the dimensions that minimize the cost.

latex.answer:
Rectangular portion frame (two heights plus bottom of length $2r$):
\begin{align*}
C_{rectangle}&=\text{cost per foot}\times \#\text{ feet}\\
&=\$10\,(h+2r+h)\\
&=\$10\,(2h+2r)\\
&=20h+20r
\end{align*}
Half-circle frame (curved boundary of length $\pi r$):
\begin{align*}
C_{halfCircle}&=\text{cost per foot}\times \#\text{ feet}\\
&=\$20\cdot \pi r
\end{align*}
Total:
\begin{align*}
C&=C_{rectangle}+C_{halfCircle}\\
C&=20h+20r+20\pi r
\end{align*}

Width of rectangle is $2r$:
\begin{align*}
A&=A_{rectangle}+A_{halfCircle}\\
&=h(2r)+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}\\
\intertext{Area must be exactly $100$ ft$^2$:}
100&=2hr+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}
\end{align*}

We minimize cost. $C$ depends on both $r$ and $h$, so use the area constraint to reduce to one variable. Solving for $h$ is easier:
\begin{align*}
2hr+\frac{\pi r^2}{2}&=100\\
2hr&=100-\frac{\pi r^2}{2}\\
h&=\frac{100-\frac{\pi r^2}{2}}{2r}\\
h&=\frac{100}{2r}-\frac{\frac{\pi r^2}{2}}{2r}\\
h&=\frac{50}{r}-\frac{\pi r}{4}
\end{align*}
Substitute into $C$:
\begin{align*}
C&=20h+20r+20\pi r\\
&=20\left(\frac{50}{r}-\frac{\pi r}{4}\right)+20r+20\pi r\\
&=\frac{1000}{r}-\frac{20\pi r}{4}+20r+20\pi r\\
&=\frac{1000}{r}-5\pi r+20r+20\pi r\\
C(r)&=\frac{1000}{r}+20r+15\pi r
\end{align*}

Domain: $r$ must be positive (a negative or zero radius makes no sense), so $0<r<\infty$. Eliminate $r=-\sqrt{\frac{200}{4+3\pi}}$ since it is not in the domain. Test $r=\sqrt{\frac{200}{4+3\pi}}\approx 3.86$.

Sign line for $C'(r)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $r=1$ (left of $3.86$): $C'(1)=-\frac{1000}{1^2}+20+15\pi\approx -932.88<0$.
\item $r=5$ (right of $3.86$): $C'(5)=-\frac{1000}{5^2}+20+15\pi=-40+20+15\pi\approx 27.12>0$.
\end{itemize}
$C'$ goes $(-)\to(+)$ at $r\approx 3.86$, so $r\approx 3.86$ is a local minimum by the First Derivative Test. Since it is the only critical number on the domain and is a local minimum, the First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema gives an absolute minimum there.

\begin{align*}
r&=\sqrt{\frac{200}{4+3\pi}}\approx 3.86\text{ feet}\\
h&=\frac{50}{r}-\frac{\pi r}{4}\\
h&=\frac{50}{\sqrt{200/(4+3\pi)}}-\frac{\pi\sqrt{200/(4+3\pi)}}{4}\approx 9.92\text{ feet}
\end{align*}
